   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT relating to giving the Malta town board the discretion to change speed limits",
    "basePrintNo": "S6679",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6679-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "",
    "lawSection": "Saratoga County",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S6679\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act relating to giving the Malta town board the\ndiscretion to change speed limits\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nThis bill would allow the Malta town board to alter the speed limits\nof roads within the town limits, provided that they have been\nclassified as local roads by the department of transportation.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1: Grants the Malta town board the discretion to establish\nmaximum speed limits on designated town highways lower than the\nfifty-five miles per hour statutory speed limit, provided that no\nlimits are placed at less than twenty-five miles per hour, except that\nschool speed limits may be placed no lower than fifteen miles per\nhour. Further allows the Malta town board to establish maximum speed\nlimits on designated town highways within such towns or designated\nareas at less than the fifty-five miles per hour statutory speed\nlimit, except that such speed limits may be placed below thirty miles\nper hour. Provides that no such speed limits will be placed outside of\nthe accordance of regulations provided by engineers and licensed\nsafety experts. Further provides that this legislation shall not alter\nthe authority of the Department of Transportation to establish maximum\nspeed limits at the request of the Malta town board\n\nSection 2: Establishes the effective date of this legislation.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nThe town of Malta has experienced an excessive number of automobile\naccidents in recent years on local roadways with speed limits that do\nnot necessarily accommodate the town's geography. This legislation\nwould allow the Malta town board the discretion to adjust speed limits\nto help reduce car accidents based upon the specific features of the\nroadways in question.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNew bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone to the State.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis bill will become effective on the one hundred eightieth day after\nit becomes law.\n",
